PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current round: C majority (1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9 = 8), B (10), D (7). Colleagues 3–6 shifted from B to C; Colleague 7 shifted from B to D. My previous C is now uncertain; I lean D based on the consumer-protection issue.

SGA track: For specific goods requiring seller repair, property/risk does not pass until the repair is actually done and the buyer has notice of actual deliverability (Ontario SGA s.20 Rule 2). The June 5 text “almost finished… will be ready June 6” is anticipatory, not notice of completion. If SGA alone governs, C is best: no post-completion notice before the flood, so risk remained with Jake. B’s practical-notice argument is possible but minority. D would be too broad under SGA because possession is not always required for risk to pass. C’s wording is awkward but the majority reads it as prospective notice not satisfying the statute.

New/critical: Jake sells refurbished computers/consoles out of his basement as a side business, advertises on Facebook Marketplace, and has inventory; Luke is a consumer. This likely makes the transaction a consumer agreement under Ontario’s Consumer Protection Act. Under the CPA, risk of loss remains with the supplier until the consumer receives/takes possession of the goods. Luke never took possession; the MacBook was destroyed before pickup. Thus Jake bears the risk and must refund. D accurately states this rule. The C majority may have missed the CPA issue. D is not merely a broad SGA rule; in a consumer transaction it is the controlling risk rule. Therefore I now favor D. If the question is intended to test only SGA, C remains strong, but the side-business facts are otherwise irrelevant and likely signal CPA. A/E are wrong. B is wrong under CPA.
}

